	affiliations = {Bina Nusantara University, Department of Management Information Systems, Jakarta, Indonesia},
	abstract = {Quick Respond Code Indonesia Standard (QRIS) is a financial technology that integrates all QR codes from each payment system service provider. However, Generation X which are often rigid and have difficulty adopting new technologies that are rapidly developing, raise new questions about whether they can adopt QRIS technology. Indonesia, as a developing country, faces challenges in terms of existing technological infrastructure, which makes financial transactions using QRIS complex, as well as challenges in financial inclusion, which result in a lack of digital knowledge and high reliance on cash. This study used purposive sampling, a non-probability sampling strategy, to collect data from 453 respondents via an online survey, and Smart PLS 4.1.08 was employed for SEM analysis. The findings of this study revealed that security plays an essential role in QRIS adoption in Generation X, where with a path coefficient value of 0.434 security demonstrating that the risks associated with using QRIS as a payment method, misuse of user information, and transaction security are all critical factors. The public will be willing to adopt QRIS if security is guaranteed by both the payment system service provider and the government, which is a supporter of QRIS infrastructure. Aside from that, this study discovered an intriguing finding: In Generation X, the convenience variable had no direct influence and was mediated by social influence. The findings of this study will also help the government and payment system service providers understand the resistance to adopting QRIS in Generation X, as well as how to develop QRIS to overcome it. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Cashless In Developing Countries; Financial Technology; Generation X; Integrated Payment; Qris},
